  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nWe will remain generally under zonal flow aloft through the \nrest of the day which will mean plenty of upslope clouds and \nisolated showers along and north of the mountains. South of the \nmountains will see mostly sunny conditions. \n\nFor tonight into Thursday morning, we transition into southwest\nflow aloft as a trough starts to dig and approach from the west.\nModels are in fairly good agreement that we should see an\ninitial wave eject into the region by Thursday afternoon,\nleading to increasing precipitation chances. As a surface low\ndevelops and lifts into the Gulf of Maine Thursday night into\nFriday, we should see multiple rounds of precipitation wrapping\naround with the heaviest rates likely across southern New \nHampshire, portions of central NH, and far southwest Maine.\nWhile models continue to come into better agreement, there are\nstill multiple scenarios on the table. The GFS and ensemble\ncontinue to be the western outlier which would result in more\nsignificant snowfall accumulations with some potential even up \nto the coast. The ECMWF, CMC, and their respective ensembles \nkeep most of the precipitation over New Hampshire and the \nsouthwest tip of Maine. The areas that we are most confident \nthat could see a few inches of accumulation will be over the \nhigher elevations (generally over 1000 feet) of New Hampshire \nand portions of western Maine. The two areas where we could see \nseveral inches of accumulation will be the Monadnock region and \nportions of the Whites. Here, NBM probabilities of at least 2 \ninches of snow are maximized around 60 to 70 percent. Increasing\nthat threshold to 4 inches or more and the highest \nprobabilities drop into the 20 to 40 percent range, maximized \nover the Monadnocks. Elsewhere across New Hampshire and western \nMaine, a few flakes could mix in at times with the rain or we \ncould see a brief dusting. \n\nMost of the guidance then has the bulk of steady precipitation \nmoving out of the area by mid to late day Friday but the \npattern will remain unsettled as the upper low is stubborn to \npush out. Thus, periodic rain and snow showers will remain\npossible through the weekend, especially across the mountains.\nWe should dry out by the end of the weekend and into the next\nwork week as high pressure settles in. Temperatures will \ngenerally remain average to slightly below average for this time\nof year through the rest of the week and into next week.",
